1
00:00:08,971 --> 00:00:12,837
So now that we've seen
basic use of Kubernetes,

2
00:00:12,837 --> 00:00:16,898
let's dive into
the architecture a little bit.

3
00:00:16,898 --> 00:00:21,679
And this will help us really understand
how networking works, because we need to

4
00:00:21,679 --> 00:00:26,835
understand kind of all the components and
the general structure of the architecture.

5
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:36,463
So here's a high level picture of
the Kubernetes architecture, right?

6
00:00:36,463 --> 00:00:40,506
So we have the control plane where it's
running a bunch of different things.

7
00:00:40,506 --> 00:00:45,002
We talked about the scheduler,
controller manager, and

8
00:00:45,002 --> 00:00:48,407
then we have a bunch of workers,
all right?

9
00:00:48,407 --> 00:00:54,111
So we talked about container runtime,
that's something like Docker and

10
00:00:54,111 --> 00:00:57,490
you can run proxies and things like that.

11
00:00:57,490 --> 00:01:00,152
So we'll dive into a bunch of these.

12
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:05,282
Now how components are run.

13
00:01:05,282 --> 00:01:12,740
So the kubelet is the only component that
always runs as a regular system component.

14
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:17,048
Kubelet then runs all
other components as pods.

15
00:01:17,048 --> 00:01:23,586
So what kubelet is, it runs on each node,
including the control plane.

16
00:01:23,586 --> 00:01:29,438
It's what can communicate with the server
when you say I need a pod created,

17
00:01:29,438 --> 00:01:35,024
kubelet is the one that will make that
happen [COUGH] on that local node.

18
00:01:35,024 --> 00:01:39,089
So it'll interact with a container
runtime like docker, but

19
00:01:39,089 --> 00:01:41,130
Docker is not the only option.

20
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:46,934
It will to create the containers and
set up the pod.

21
00:01:46,934 --> 00:01:50,804
So that's what runs as a system component,
but

22
00:01:50,804 --> 00:01:54,300
all of these other components run as pods.

23
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:59,766
So let's explore this a little bit.

24
00:01:59,766 --> 00:02:04,108
All right, so
let me pull open our terminal.

25
00:02:04,108 --> 00:02:08,345
We can run docker ps, see our nodes,

26
00:02:08,345 --> 00:02:13,052
let's log into the control plane node.

27
00:02:13,052 --> 00:02:16,386
So docker exec -it,

28
00:02:16,386 --> 00:02:21,018
give it the container name and

29
00:02:21,018 --> 00:02:25,101
we'll say /bin/bash.

30
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:31,390
All right, I could say ps aux,
I could see a bunch of things like

31
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:36,031
the kube-scheduler,
kube-controller, kube-apiserver.

32
00:02:36,031 --> 00:02:40,477
But let's use the, so
inside of the container,

33
00:02:40,477 --> 00:02:45,038
the container runtime in
this case is not docker.

34
00:02:45,038 --> 00:02:52,569
And so to interact with it, I'm saying
crictl and I can say ps, all right?

35
00:02:52,569 --> 00:02:58,694
And so here are the various components,
the containers or

36
00:02:58,694 --> 00:03:04,950
pods that are running inside
of the control pit plane node.

37
00:03:08,827 --> 00:03:15,008
So that's looking at it for the
perspective of the container runtime fFrom

38
00:03:15,008 --> 00:03:20,121
the perspective of Kubernetes,
I could say kubectl get all.

39
00:03:20,121 --> 00:03:23,772
And when I do that it's not
going to show much, but

40
00:03:23,772 --> 00:03:27,435
if I add -a,
it'll start showing something.

41
00:03:27,435 --> 00:03:33,310
So this is take a quick detour
to mention what a namespace is.

42
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:38,621
So in namespace, in Kubernetes,

43
00:03:38,621 --> 00:03:43,523
that's basically
a grouping of resources in

44
00:03:43,523 --> 00:03:47,784
a particular partition or isolation.

45
00:03:47,784 --> 00:03:53,597
So they're all running, but, so
the default namespace when we say kubectl,

46
00:03:53,597 --> 00:03:58,187
apply and then create a pod,
that's the default namespace.

47
00:03:58,187 --> 00:04:02,139
But there's other namespaces,

48
00:04:02,139 --> 00:04:07,260
kubectl get namespaces that exist.

49
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:13,180
And where a lot of the system components
get run is within this kube system.

50
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:17,184
So if I say kubectl get all -a,

51
00:04:17,184 --> 00:04:22,400
I'm saying for all namespaces.

52
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:28,648
All right, and so
we could see the various components.

53
00:04:28,648 --> 00:04:32,995
So here's all the pods
that are running and

54
00:04:32,995 --> 00:04:40,976
we could say -o wide if we want to see
where their extra information about them.

55
00:04:40,976 --> 00:04:45,334
All right, kube system, so

56
00:04:45,334 --> 00:04:49,510
this is kind of one entry and

57
00:04:49,510 --> 00:04:53,687
you can kind of see all the,

58
00:04:53,687 --> 00:04:58,600
well sorry, this is one entry.

59
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:05,612
So you can see this component is
running on the node control plane,

60
00:05:05,612 --> 00:05:08,470
has this IP address and so forth.

61
00:05:08,470 --> 00:05:10,850
So there's a lot of
components that are running.

62
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:12,831
So explore that a little bit on your own.

63
00:05:15,265 --> 00:05:18,113
All right, so
let's dive into some of these components.

64
00:05:18,113 --> 00:05:22,269
One that you might have
seen is called etcd.

65
00:05:22,269 --> 00:05:26,580
What etcs is,
is a distributed key value store.

66
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:30,848
So it stores all the state for
a Kubernetes cluster,

67
00:05:30,848 --> 00:05:35,310
information about the pods,
about the deployments.

68
00:05:35,310 --> 00:05:40,329
And it uses the RAFT
consensus algorithm to ensure

69
00:05:40,329 --> 00:05:45,827
consensus because this is
run in a replicated manner,

70
00:05:45,827 --> 00:05:49,188
it'll be run on multiple nodes.

71
00:05:49,188 --> 00:05:54,086
So this is showing just two, and when
anything kind of reads or writes to or

72
00:05:54,086 --> 00:05:58,596
writes to one of the nodes,
it'll synchronize to another node.

73
00:05:58,596 --> 00:06:03,029
So the RAFT algorithm will ensure
that each node state is either what

74
00:06:03,029 --> 00:06:06,528
the majority of the nodes
agree is the current state or

75
00:06:06,528 --> 00:06:09,513
is one of the previously
agreed upon states.

76
00:06:11,546 --> 00:06:16,091
So that's the property of etcd and
it's a system that exists,

77
00:06:16,091 --> 00:06:21,520
you can go it's not only used in
Kubernetes, but it is used in Kubernetes.

78
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:28,486
Next we can mention the API Server and
this provides a CRUD interface or

79
00:06:28,486 --> 00:06:33,435
create, read, update,
delete interface for querying and

80
00:06:33,435 --> 00:06:37,138
modifying the cluster
state over a REST API.

81
00:06:37,138 --> 00:06:40,356
And then it stores all the state in etcd.

82
00:06:40,356 --> 00:06:45,508
So as we mentioned, we're interacting with
the Kubernetes cluster, with kubectl.

83
00:06:45,508 --> 00:06:48,900
So that's just a client
of this API server.

84
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:53,688
So it'll send request REST API
through the REST API.

85
00:06:53,688 --> 00:06:58,068
And then there's the series
of kind of things that

86
00:06:58,068 --> 00:07:02,986
perform authentication which
we don't have enabled.

87
00:07:02,986 --> 00:07:08,106
Does some admission control which can
do things like modifying resources,

88
00:07:08,106 --> 00:07:13,456
initializing missing fields, and so
forth, and then resource validation.

89
00:07:13,456 --> 00:07:19,084
Once it gets past all that it goes into
etcd and says great, now we've got

90
00:07:19,084 --> 00:07:24,271
a request to create a resource or
updates the state in some manner.

91
00:07:26,010 --> 00:07:30,655
Now how that then interacts with
all the other components is all of

92
00:07:30,655 --> 00:07:34,932
the other components are just
watching on the API server.

93
00:07:34,932 --> 00:07:40,674
So the components open an HTTP session and
then say watch = true.

94
00:07:40,674 --> 00:07:45,120
So this means they're basically
setting themselves up as a listener.

95
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:47,868
So now anytime a command,
so that's the first step,

96
00:07:47,868 --> 00:07:50,086
each of these components would do that.

97
00:07:50,086 --> 00:07:56,436
Now let's say kubectl does something
that it does a post, it creates a pod.

98
00:07:56,436 --> 00:08:02,354
Well, API server, as we mentioned,
will send to etcd update the state.

99
00:08:02,354 --> 00:08:08,464
etcd will send a modification
notification to the API server,

100
00:08:08,464 --> 00:08:14,584
which will then send the updated
object to all of the watchers.

101
00:08:14,584 --> 00:08:21,451
We can also have kubectl itself
watch if we ran that command.

102
00:08:24,632 --> 00:08:26,640
Next, the scheduler.

103
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:32,530
So the scheduler waits for
newly created pods,

104
00:08:32,530 --> 00:08:36,624
then it assigns a node to each new pod.

105
00:08:36,624 --> 00:08:41,944
So it doesn't actually instruct
the selected node to run the pod,

106
00:08:41,944 --> 00:08:47,169
it just updates the state of the pod
that it goes from not scheduled

107
00:08:47,169 --> 00:08:52,869
to it selects a node, updates the state
to say it should run on node two,

108
00:08:52,869 --> 00:08:57,840
and then updates the state
to say that it's scheduled.

109
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:03,979
So the basic operation of
the scheduler is a multi-step process.

110
00:09:03,979 --> 00:09:09,923
And it's quite interesting and you can
highly recommend you kind of dig in and

111
00:09:09,923 --> 00:09:13,780
read it if there's something
of interest to you.

112
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:15,640
But it'll first filter, so

113
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:20,352
it'll find the set of nodes where
it's feasible to schedule the pod.

114
00:09:20,352 --> 00:09:24,826
So you can specify for
example using labels and say,

115
00:09:24,826 --> 00:09:29,415
I want this to run on any
node that matches this label.

116
00:09:29,415 --> 00:09:32,269
So it'll filter out any that it doesn't or

117
00:09:32,269 --> 00:09:36,300
maybe there's nodes that are down and
not ready.

118
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:41,127
Then it'll perform a scoring
of all the nodes that made it

119
00:09:41,127 --> 00:09:46,344
past the filter to choose
the most suitable pod placement.

120
00:09:46,344 --> 00:09:51,115
And this is broken down into a bunch of
extension points where you can kind of

121
00:09:51,115 --> 00:09:52,720
look through.

122
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:58,270
And there's a lot of
interesting things in that.

123
00:09:58,270 --> 00:10:02,975
The controller manager
component runs multiple

124
00:10:02,975 --> 00:10:07,450
controllers as processes
performing various

125
00:10:07,450 --> 00:10:12,928
reconciliation loop tasks or
reconciliation loops.

126
00:10:12,928 --> 00:10:17,661
So we have replication manager replication
manager, ReplicaSet, DaemonSet,

127
00:10:17,661 --> 00:10:19,690
Deployment, Node, Service.

128
00:10:19,690 --> 00:10:23,598
So we talked about
the deployment last time.

129
00:10:23,598 --> 00:10:30,395
So this will run as a process within the
controller manager and it'll be watching.

130
00:10:30,395 --> 00:10:34,898
All right, for example,
did something change, did pod,

131
00:10:34,898 --> 00:10:40,136
the configuration change or
the number of pods and things like that.

132
00:10:42,635 --> 00:10:45,262
[COUGH] So
it will watch the API Server for

133
00:10:45,262 --> 00:10:51,220
changes to resources they care about and
perform operations for each change.

134
00:10:51,220 --> 00:10:55,327
So the ReplicaSet watches,
ReplicaSet resources and

135
00:10:55,327 --> 00:10:59,620
Pod resources, and creates and
deletes Pod resources.

136
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:04,117
Endpoints, watch Services resources and
Pod resources and

137
00:11:04,117 --> 00:11:06,920
creates Endpoint resources.

138
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:10,251
And each of these runs
a reconciliation loop, so

139
00:11:10,251 --> 00:11:14,574
you can actually look inside
of the Kubernetes source code.

140
00:11:14,574 --> 00:11:18,678
There's a directory controller that
you can look at all of these and

141
00:11:18,678 --> 00:11:20,557
basically see that in action.

142
00:11:23,251 --> 00:11:26,615
Next that we'll talk about is kubelet.

143
00:11:26,615 --> 00:11:31,708
So we've briefly mentioned this,
but it'll register the node

144
00:11:31,708 --> 00:11:37,060
it's running on by creating a node
resource in the API server.

145
00:11:37,060 --> 00:11:43,698
It watches the API server for
Pods that have been scheduled to its node.

146
00:11:43,698 --> 00:11:46,143
So if you recall,
the scheduler doesn't do that.

147
00:11:46,143 --> 00:11:49,821
So the scheduler just
updates the state and

148
00:11:49,821 --> 00:11:54,533
kubelet will see that this
Pod state has been updated.

149
00:11:54,533 --> 00:11:56,490
So that's what it's watching on.

150
00:11:56,490 --> 00:12:01,440
It'll then start the Pods containers
by telling the container runtime to run

151
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:03,684
a container from a given image.

152
00:12:03,684 --> 00:12:08,220
Then it monitors the running containers,
reports any events.

153
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:14,002
It runs liveness probes,
restarts containers when the probes fail,

154
00:12:14,002 --> 00:12:18,715
and then deletes the containers
when the Pod is deleted.

155
00:12:22,362 --> 00:12:26,628
It can also run things called static Pods.

156
00:12:26,628 --> 00:12:31,412
There's a special
directory that you can say,

157
00:12:31,412 --> 00:12:36,435
here are some Pods I want to
create automatically,

158
00:12:36,435 --> 00:12:39,916
statically as part of the system.

159
00:12:39,916 --> 00:12:43,545
Right, so let's look at a kind of
summary of the chain of events.

160
00:12:43,545 --> 00:12:48,592
Right, so let's say in kubectl we say,
I want to create some resource.

161
00:12:48,592 --> 00:12:51,652
Here, I'm going to create a deployment,
right?

162
00:12:51,652 --> 00:12:57,258
So we're going to kind of match this
as etcd and API server, all of this.

163
00:12:57,258 --> 00:13:01,266
So I come in and
I say create a deployment.

164
00:13:01,266 --> 00:13:06,287
So in etcd and the API server will
notify the resource controller and

165
00:13:06,287 --> 00:13:11,939
the particular process, the deployment
controller that's watching it.

166
00:13:11,939 --> 00:13:16,214
The deployment controller
will update the ReplicaSet,

167
00:13:16,214 --> 00:13:20,051
ReplicaSet will then,
that's all gets updated.

168
00:13:20,051 --> 00:13:24,114
The ReplicaSet controller
will see that and

169
00:13:24,114 --> 00:13:28,723
it will update the Pod,
create Pods and whatnot,

170
00:13:28,723 --> 00:13:34,928
which once it creates this Pod resource,
it'll be unscheduled.

171
00:13:34,928 --> 00:13:38,866
So then the scheduler which is
watching on Pods sees that and

172
00:13:38,866 --> 00:13:41,796
says, this Pod needs to be scheduled.

173
00:13:41,796 --> 00:13:45,900
It'll figure out which node,
it'll update that state.

174
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:50,624
And then once that state is updated,
the kubelet is watching on that and

175
00:13:50,624 --> 00:13:53,224
it will contact the local runtime which

176
00:13:53,224 --> 00:13:57,520
could be Docker which will
then create the containers.

177
00:13:57,520 --> 00:14:04,823
So that's a summary of a chain of events
when you're creating a deployment.

178
00:14:07,064 --> 00:14:10,457
So kind of summarizing,
showing this picture again,

179
00:14:10,457 --> 00:14:13,186
we've talked about the API server, etcd,

180
00:14:13,186 --> 00:14:18,076
the scheduler, controller manager,
kubelet, and the container runtime.

181
00:14:18,076 --> 00:14:19,515
We've talked plenty about.

182
00:14:22,369 --> 00:14:28,880
So next time we're going to go into
the networking aspect of Kubernetes.

183
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:35,126
But now that we have the overarching
context of the architecture in mind,

184
00:14:35,126 --> 00:14:38,944
we can now better understand networking.

185
00:14:38,944 --> 00:14:42,035
And then ultimately we can write our own

186
00:14:42,035 --> 00:14:45,790
networking constructs
inside of Kubernetes.

187
00:14:45,790 --> 00:14:47,880
And with that, we'll end here, thanks.